B1.3 – Call Count Experiment
Aim
To compare the number of recursive calls made by linear power and fast power for different values of n.
C code
Add this experiment to your B1.c program (or replace the previous main() with this one).

int main(void)
{
    int values[] = {10, 100, 1000, 10000};
    int count = 4;

    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
    {
        int n = values[i];

        calls = 0;
        power_linear(1, n);
        long long linearCalls = calls;

        calls = 0;
        power_fast(1, n);
        long long fastCalls = calls;

        printf("n = %d\n", n);
        printf("Linear calls = %lld\n", linearCalls);
        printf("Fast calls = %lld\n\n", fastCalls);
    }

    return 0;
}

Expected call counts
n Linear calls Fast calls
10 11 5
100 101 8
1000 1001 11
10000 10001 15
Conclusion
Linear power makes n + 1 recursive calls, so its time complexity is O(n). Fast power divides the exponent by 2 in every recursive call, so its time complexity is O(log n). Fast power is more efficient for large exponents.

### B1.4 – Recursion Limit Test

Linear recursion uses more function calls as `n` increases. For large values, it may cause a stack overflow.

Fast recursion uses fewer calls and is more efficient.

Conclusion: Iterative methods are safer for very large inputs because they use less stack memory.
